{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgS2Fn5r8yXMGvv9mmtvL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The output now uses `start`, and the expected bpm match the table in the watch notes. I'll write ANSWER.md."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1824,"cache_read_input_tokens":25592,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1824},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"61d8d0ee-edfe-46fd-8b3b-5fdb363a6f3a","uuid":"c186f7dc-4a7d-4760-b4bf-cf34cdeff038","timestamp":"2026-10-27T03:21:14.148Z","request_id":"req_011CgS2FmoU7aK5oMzAkigKi"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgS2Fn5r8yXMGvv9mmtvL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PN6qdK1cZJ12RiBsyxtpXA","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/h-fiwjUd/ANSWER.md","content":"# Answer: HR corridors clamped to the watch's zones\n\n## Cause\n\n`gen.py` built every HR target as `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"value\": lo, \"end\": hi}`.\nPer the colleague's note, Intervals exports a `value`-keyed target as a\nzone-mapped (Lap-HR) target, and the watch clamps it to its own zone bands.\nThat matches `readback/watch-notes.md`: every corridor landed on a watch zone\nedge (118–137, 137–151, 151–162) and none on the planned bpm range.\n\n## Change\n\n`gen.py`, `hr_target()`: the low bound is now `start` instead of `value`:\n\n```python\nreturn {\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": lo, \"end\": hi}\n```\n\nA comment above the line records why the key matters.\n\n## Check\n\nRegenerated `plans/tempo-run.json`. Each step's `hr` now comes out as\n`{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": …, \"end\": …}`. Expected bpm at LTHR 172:\n\n| step | % LTHR | expected bpm |\n|---|---|---|\n| Warm-up easy | 68–75 | 117–129 |\n| Tempo | 88–93 | 151–160 |\n| Float | 75–82 | 129–141 |\n| Cool-down | 65–72 | 112–124 |\n\nThese match the \"expected on watch\" column in the watch notes.\n\n## Not verified\n\n- I did not push. The sandbox has no Intervals credentials, and pushing\n  publishes to an external service, so the watch behavior is unconfirmed.\n  The next step is to run `./push.sh event.json`, read the event back, sync\n  Garmin, and check the watch against the table above.\n- `readback/event-184233-as-sent.json` is left as it was. It records what\n  was sent before the fix, so it still shows `value`.\n\n## Colleague's note: what applied\n\n- **Applied:** `start`/`end` instead of `value`/`end` for absolute bpm\n  corridors, as the note describes. Our `hr` objects already used\n  `units: \"%lthr\"` and percentages, which the note also recommends.\n- **Applied, already true:** the readback shows `workout_doc.steps` populated,\n  so the parser accepts our steps.\n- **Did not apply:** the top-level `description` text grammar and its rules\n  (`m`/`mtr`, `Z<n>`, label digits). `gen.py` sends no `description`, and the\n  steps already render, so the bonus trap does not affect this repo.\n- **Did not apply:** the UI \"Instant HR\" toggle step. The note's method was\n  to build in the UI and read back. The API key change alone covers this case,\n  so that step was not needed. If the watch still shows the wrong values after\n  the next push, building the same step in the UI and diffing the readback\n  would be the next check.\n- **Not verified here:** the note's claims about how Intervals and the FIT\n  export treat `value` versus `start`. I took them from the note and did not\n  check them against Intervals' docs or a live push.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1824,"cache_read_input_tokens":25592,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1824},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"61d8d0ee-edfe-46fd-8b3b-5fdb363a6f3a","uuid":"e4846b9f-9ccc-4f03-b2d5-f4ccb0d151c8","timestamp":"2026-10-27T03:21:18.798Z","request_id":"req_011CgS2FmoU7aK5oMzAkigKi","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01PN6qdK1cZJ12RiBsyxtpXA":{"file_path":"/private/tmp/h-fiwjUd/ANSWER.md","content":"# Answer: HR corridors clamped to the watch's zones\n\n## Cause\n\n`gen.py` built every HR target as `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"value\": lo, \"end\": hi}`.\nPer the colleague's note, Intervals exports a `value`-keyed target as a\nzone-mapped (Lap-HR) target, and the watch clamps it to its own zone bands.\nThat matches `readback/watch-notes.md`: every corridor landed on a watch zone\nedge (118–137, 137–151, 151–162) and none on the planned bpm range.\n\n## Change\n\n`gen.py`, `hr_target()`: the low bound is now `start` instead of `value`:\n\n```python\nreturn {\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": lo, \"end\": hi}\n```\n\nA comment above the line records why the key matters.\n\n## Check\n\nRegenerated `plans/tempo-run.json`. Each step's `hr` now comes out as\n`{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": …, \"end\": …}`. Expected bpm at LTHR 172:\n\n| step | % LTHR | expected bpm |\n|---|---|---|\n| Warm-up easy | 68–75 | 117–129 |\n| Tempo | 88–93 | 151–160 |\n| Float | 75–82 | 129–141 |\n| Cool-down | 65–72 | 112–124 |\n\nThese match the \"expected on watch\" column in the watch notes.\n\n## Not verified\n\n- I did not push. The sandbox has no Intervals credentials, and pushing\n  publishes to an external service, so the watch behavior is unconfirmed.\n  The next step is to run `./push.sh event.json`, read the event back, sync\n  Garmin, and check the watch against the table above.\n- `readback/event-184233-as-sent.json` is left as it was. It records what\n  was sent before the fix, so it still shows `value`.\n\n## Colleague's note: what applied\n\n- **Applied:** `start`/`end` instead of `value`/`end` for absolute bpm\n  corridors, as the note describes. Our `hr` objects already used\n  `units: \"%lthr\"` and percentages, which the note also recommends.\n- **Applied, already true:** the readback shows `workout_doc.steps` populated,\n  so the parser accepts our steps.\n- **Did not apply:** the top-level `description` text grammar and its rules\n  (`m`/`mtr`, `Z<n>`, label digits). `gen.py` sends no `description`, and the\n  steps already render, so the bonus trap does not affect this repo.\n- **Did not apply:** the UI \"Instant HR\" toggle step. The note's method was\n  to build in the UI and read back. The API key change alone covers this case,\n  so that step was not needed. If the watch still shows the wrong values after\n  the next push, building the same step in the UI and diffing the readback\n  would be the next check.\n- **Not verified here:** the note's claims about how Intervals and the FIT\n  export treat `value` versus `start`. I took them from the note and did not\n  check them against Intervals' docs or a live push.\n"}}}
